{"author":{"id":"a3a282966ced55effb4560271fcd98e13241af24ea76b00ec76a0f18154bc8ee"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790976673,"nonce":"x4C1sXIUIuG5v1WR5ZsRxN8VAa8=","message":"Landed on branch `worktree-agent-ac69fb8557d5f974b` (`92f532d8`), design `doc/design/report.md` (`286cdf79`).\n\n`entities/issue.SnapshotAt(ops, at)` replays the operations whose own wall-clock time is at or before `at`, in the DAG's order, with the issue package's own `Apply`: the apply rules keep one implementation. `OperationsBetween(ops, from, to)` is its window twin, half-open. Both live in `entities/issue` because that package owns the rules; `Compile` is the case where nothing is filtered.\n\nTwo behaviors fall out of the replay rather than being coded: an issue whose create operation is later than `at` is absent (`get --at` says \"did not exist at ...\", the list leaves it out), and `archived` is the value that stood then, so the list's default `select(.fields.archived != true)` filters on it with no special case. Operations are filtered one by one, not truncated at the first late one, because wall clocks across clones are not monotonic in DAG order.\n\nCommands: `issue get ID --at TIME`, `issue [PROGRAM] --at TIME`, `issue log [ID|PROGRAM] [--from TIME] [--to TIME]`. Host: `IssueGetAt`, `IssueSnapshotAt`, `IssueListAt`, `IssueListInputAt`, `IssueLogBetween`; the old names are thin wrappers passing the zero time.\n\nCalls made:\n\n- ID vs PROGRAM on the log: resolve as an id prefix or alias first; what does not resolve is compiled as a jq program; if that fails too the error names both attempts, so a mistyped id reads as a mistyped id. No `--query` flag -- an id prefix is lowercase hex and no bare hex word is a valid jq program, so the ambiguity is not real. The empty argument is the default program, as in the list.\n- The excerpt at a time is built from the replayed snapshot into the same `cmdjson.IssueExcerpt` shape (`NewIssueExcerptAt`), so a jq program written for the present reads the past unchanged. `cache/` is untouched: it holds the present only. `edit_time.lamport` is omitted -- a lamport time belongs to the entity, not to an operation -- while `edit_time.timestamp` and the whole of `create_time` are exact.\n- `IssueOperation` gains `issue` (the id it belongs to) and `time` (the same moment as `unix_time`, written out as RFC 3339, so a flow can print a date without owning a calendar). Both are present whether one issue was asked for or many.\n- Ordering across issues is operation time ascending, ties broken by issue id and then by position within the issue.\n- TIME is one grammar -- a date, an RFC 3339 time, or a duration back from now -- in `host.ParseTime`. `host.ParseDuration` is the parser `jira sync --adopt` already used, moved up and given `w`; `commands/jira` calls it and keeps its own `--adopt:` wording.\n- Starlark: `work.issue.get(id, at=...)`, `work.issue.list(program, at=...)`, `work.issue.log(id_or_program, from_=..., to=...)`. `from_` with a trailing underscore because `from` is a reserved word, the second instance of the `import_` rule. `None` and the empty string both mean \"not given\", so a flow passes its own optional argument straight through.\n- Performance: `--at` is linear in the store's operations -- every issue replayed once, in memory, no index. Fine for hundreds; a full pass per call beyond that. Recorded in the design.\n\nTests: `host/issue_at_test.go` (replay at three times, created-after absent, archived-then honored by the list's default, excerpt shape, half-open window, selection errors, the TIME grammar); `commands/issue/issue_at_test.go` (the flags, the text forms, flag-naming refusals); `flow/run/run_test.go` (TestIssueHistoryKwargs, TestIssueHistoryKwargsRefusals). gofmt, vet and the whole test suite pass.\n\nAGENTS.md and `doc/design/cli-convention.md` carry the flags and the Starlark mirror.","files":null}]}